From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bert Belder <bertbelder@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Allow placing an IsolateGroup sandbox in an embedder reservation

With the sandbox and multiple pointer compression cages enabled, every
v8::IsolateGroup::Create() reserves its own sandbox at an address that
V8 picks at random. Embedders that manage the address space themselves
(for example to apply hardware memory isolation per isolate group) need
to decide where each sandbox lives.

Add v8::IsolateGroup::CreateParams with an optional embedder-owned
reservation and v8::IsolateGroup::Create(const CreateParams&). When a
reservation is supplied, the group's sandbox is placed at its start:
Sandbox::InitializeAt() adopts the memory through a new
base::VirtualAddressSpace::AdoptSubspace(), which wraps an existing
inaccessible private mapping in a VirtualAddressSubspace that does not
own (and therefore never unmaps) the reservation. Inside the subspace V8
keeps remapping pages in place with MAP_FIXED as it does for its own
reservations, so the pointer compression cage, guard regions and page
allocator work unchanged.

The reservation must be aligned to kSandboxAlignment and must be at
least kSandboxSize + 2 * kSandboxGuardRegionSize +
kAdditionalTrailingGuardRegionSize bytes. An adopted sandbox is always
fully reserved with guard regions; there is no partially-reserved
fallback. Adoption is only available on POSIX platforms other than
Fuchsia (V8_CAN_ADOPT_ADDRESS_SPACE_RESERVATION); elsewhere supplying a
reservation aborts. The existing Create() path is unchanged.

